Nope, you don't get off that easy.  I haven't even *started* on "math is
random" yet.  *My* contention is the result fails to be interesting because
(as stated) the omega set doesn't cover more than a vanishingly tiny subset
of the space of all WFFs. It is certainly possible that I have misunderstood
Costi's explanation, but you haven't even claimed that yet, much less argued
it.

If you really understand Chaitin's result, you ought to be able to defend it.
My suspicion that you do *not* actually understand it is fuelled by the fact
that your reply mostly quotes more authorities.

In fact, you're beginning to sound rather like Pandit Singh...
 
>                                                          (Not to argue from
> "authority," but Chaitin's work is very well-regarded and has not been
> disproved so far.)

I'm even beginning to wonder about that.  I hadn't heard of the omega theorem
before this, and as an ex-metamathematician I keep my ear to the ground on
these things.

You should consider the possibility that you've been taken in by either
Chaitin's over-selling of his own result, or Costi's misinterpretation of
it, or both.  I don't dispute that the omega theorem asserts something
nontrivial (the existence of a countable infinity of undecideables is
interesting) nor that the proof method is clever (it is, very).  But none
of your excerpts have justified Chaitin's claims for its significance.

I am also rather disappointed in *you*.

You yourself have observed that "extraordinary claims call for extraordinary
evidence".  *You* have made the extraordinary claim in this debate.  It's
*your* responsibility to back it up or withdraw it.

Telling the rest of us to go off and read Chaitin before you'll discuss the
matter further would be fine if you had ever actually addressed the
counterarguments and we were disagreeing on a technical point of the
proof or interpretation.  But you haven't --- and that smells bad.
-- 
