> years. I have no idea yet how to even start on it.

Costi, quoted by Tim, did the presenting of Chaitin's result.  I've been
criticizing the interpretation of that result.

What you're intepreting is *my* concept of what a stronger result than Godel's
would look like, which I presented during the argument.  And you're quite
right; if I had a natural topology on the space of WFFs, the next thing
I'd try would be to find a measure (though I delibarately didn't use that term
in previous discussion).

The strongest possible result would be that the set of decideables is of
measure zero.  I don't think I believe that, but I wouldn't be at all
surprised if the set of decideables turned out to be sparse.

Yes, it would be nice to work on, and I agree that it would probably earn
somebody a Fields Prize.  Unfortunately, my math chops have nearly fifteen
years of rust on them.  Tenure at Princeton seems unlikely. :-)
