Date: Tue, 18 May 93 13:08:49 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Chaitin is not Extropianly Correct

Tim seems to feel strongly on this issue, so let me clarify my
position.  I do not disagree with Chaitin's technical conclusions,
just reluctant to approve of using the word "random" to describe the
results.  Not having reviewed the literature as carefully as Tim, I
will back off and just comment on what I think it would take for the
"random" to be appropriate here.

I would say some set of values are "random" if the distribution of
values are predicted well by some simple statistical model, but any
attempt to predict better with a more complex model seems destined to
failure.  So microscopic parameters are "random" at thermodynamic
equilibrium, as are the outcomes of a quantum measurement.  On the
other hand, even though the distribution of human heights seems to fit
a simple distribution, one can predict heights better and better by
conditioning on more and more context information.

So, my question for this context would be, is "whether particular
exponential diophantine equations have finitely or infinitely many
solutions" predicted well by some simple statistical model?  Are 1/3
of them finite, or what?  

Robin Hanson

