Date: Tue, 18 May 93 16:21:37 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Chaitin

Well, I've read some Chaitin (easier than I thought, but then I've
been interested in similar things for some time).  This note is
one big response to the whole thread, making points on diverse topics
(though I'm saving logical tilings and other explorations of formal
systems for another time [and think it a bit more interesting than the
rather specific technical point being discussed here]).  This note
presents my views, not anybody else's.

Also, I did not have the inclination or ability to check Chatin's
proofs line by line.  Sue me.

Points:

First, Tim did not do a very good job responding to the other
posts requesting clarification.  Obviously, he is not obligated
to do so; however, I hope he wasn't surprised that people were
a bit put off, when relatively brief answers to rather specific
points were probably sometimes possible.  Still, on the Platonic
extropians(TM) list the thread would have been more collegial in
tone.

Don't talk to me about "fundamental importance" in number theory.
I don't know what it means; I think it's quite possible that even
when different people DO come to common understanding about what
a result in NT means, one could say "cool!" and another say "yawn!"
Why was Godel's Theorem fundamentally important?  Because it trashed
Hilbert's program?

I will try to present my interpretation of Chaitin's stuff, and
my interpretation of the resulting conclusion that "math is random".
Chaitin's result is not earth-shattering in the sense that some
complainers have wanted, *from the perspective of traditional number
theory*.  It's perfectly possible and reasonable to shrug it all off
with a "so what"?  However, even from that perspective, the work is of
some (large?) importance:
 * It provides an economical encoding of flexible formulations of
   godel's theorem (as have others), that has the neat ability
   to generate infinite numbers of uncomputable propositions with
   definite answers, one-upping godel in a minor but interesting way.
 * The method for computing the number Omega, which is the probability
   that a random turing machine will halt, is quite important and
   interesting (I hope somebody hacks on its efficiency and gives it
   some healthy CPU time; I'm curious!  note: lower bound of 127/128...).
 * Even more strange and wonderful:  a binary expansion of Omega contains
   as many 1's as 0's (!) and that expansion is INCOMPRESSIBLE; that is,
   it is its own shortest description.  No shorter computer program can
   be devised to compute it.  That's really weird.

In fact, that's really really weird and is the source of the claim that
"math is random".  As far as I know, there was no previous example of
such a thing.  The digits of Omega are perfectly well-defined, meaningful,
logical, expressible, etc and there is NO PATTERN TO THEM.  NONE.  No
axiom system that describes them or can derive them (succinctly),
nothing.  "Pure" math like number theory isn't "supposed" to be like
that!  This is what he means by "limitations of the axiomatic method".

Some people misinterpreted the material presented to say that just
the generation of lots of undecidable propositions was the basis
for the "math is random" conclusion.  Not so:  it's the random
nature of the expansion of Omega that's the kicker.

I see some rather less clear implications, which Chaitin seems to
be aiming at.  First, how do we study entities like Omega if the
axiomatic method offers no help at all (despite Omega's "platonic"
existence)?  Chaitin says that tools need to be developed for
studying such areas of forumula-space (or whatever) statistically.
Note that this is very different than the usual modus operandi of
the number theorist.  I understand why Tim gets excited by this;
in k-space as well as theorem-space any helpful tools for charting
the territory are extremely welcome.

However, this is speculation of nearly wormhole-order.  Before any
of that is clear at all, two things have to happen:
  * 1) larger regions need to be shown to have interesting statistical
    structure; before Chaitin, it's unclear that anybody would have had
    any inclination to _look_, but that by itself doesn't mean such
    structure (where statistics is the tool of choice) is widespread.
  * 2) appropriate statistical tools need to be developed and applied
    in useful ways.  The most powerful, Chaitin's own algorithmic
    information theory, goes partway, but only part.  And it needs
    to be more broadly applied.  [this isn't criticism of Chaitin,
    after all he just started...]

The second, more radical, implication is that Chaitin is explicitly
trying to make math an experimental science, in a way that is both
intriguing and dangerous.  If there's this randomness all through
math anyway, and seemingly unprovable statements coming out of our
ears, such as Fermat's Last Theorem (and if standard axiomitizations
may be inconsistent anyway -- a point that I didn't see Chaitin make
but I believe to be the case), let's get a little bit looser and
start just accepting new axioms and seeing what happens, like the
physicists do.

I'm unsure exactly how I feel about this swashbuckling attitude toward
math -- but it sure seems extropian to me!  

One final point:  Eric pointed out that the "omega set" does not
deal with all WFFs.  I believe this to be correct.  I understand
his disappointment; a similar-style result for all WFFs would be
completely astonishing!  Chaitin's current work aims a little
lower than that.

Comments?  Have I become worthy to participate in further discussion? :-)

derek
