The constellation of related ideas swirling around is of prime interest in
how the Extropians outlook evolves:

* physics of information
* complexity and compressibility
* algorithmic information theory
* evolutionary systems and complexity
* artificial life
* dissipative systems and complexity

cf. the books I've cited before (all are recent, circa 1988-1993): 

- Zurek's "Compexity, Entropy and the Physics of Information"
- Leff and Rex's "Maxwell's Demon: Entropy, Information, Computing" 
- Herken's "The Universal Turing Machine: A Half Century Survey
- "Workshop on Physics and Computation," PhysComp '92, Oct. 1992
- Chaitin's books and articles
- the various "popularizations" cited (which is something of a misnomer, as
both Pagels and Casti are/were active researchers)

I'd urge people on this list to be more careful about immediately
dismissing what they don't understand, or haven't taken the time to think
about. Intuition is a dangerous thing...to many, the whole idea of
"reversible computations" is self-evidently wrong ("it doesn't smell
right"), just as theories of quantum mechanics, continental drift,
relativity, and even evolution struck some as intuitively wrong. I get the
very same "ignorant skepticism" (and by this I mean absolutely no criticsm
of Eric, Robin, Ray, or Mike....this just describes the phenomenon
succinctly) routinely on the Cypherpunks list--and occasionally on this
list--when someone claims that "zero knowledge," for example, is
intuitively suspicious. Rather than writing a customized essay for them,
explaining the origins of zero knowledge and straightening them out on
their misapprehensions, I usually refer them to any of the several good
popularizations (as a starting point, these can't be beat) and "Scientific
American"-style articles.

When someone says something I don't understand, but they have provided
sufficient references for me to pursue (or have included a long excerp from
someone else's explanation), I first try to find the references and read
them. I don't first ask them spend a few hours writing a customized essay
addressing my specific areas of off-the-cuff doubt!

Derek's approach is admirable. Whether he agrees or disagrees with my
assessment of Chaitin's work is beside the point (though we do seem to
agree). The real point is that only through some education and background
reading can people speak the same language and thus communicate.

